# Ogevia production release package (stages A, B, C1, D1-D3, E1)

Source commit @COMMIT@. **Nothing in this package has been run against AWS.** It was tested locally against a mocked `aws`, a real PostgreSQL 16 (the 46 to 85 migration and its rollback), fake `cdk`/`vite` programs, and a real `cdk synth` and `vite build`. **Stages A, B and E1 are read-only. C1, D1, D2 (local), D3 and the guard `deploy` mode are the YELLOW steps below: each needs the owner's explicit GO for the window, and each stops at a typed phrase.**

## Why a discovery stage first
The production stacks (`Ogevia-prd-*`) were last deployed on 2026-10-10. What they run today (the Lambda's build, the web's version and API host, the live templates) was never recorded. A gate that allows "only the expected difference" cannot be written until the live templates are read. This stage captures them, reports how they differ from the release templates bundled here, and makes the recovery copies a release needs (the Lambda code zip, the previous code asset, the whole web bucket). The next stage (gate, window, deploy, verify) is written from this report.

## Release under review
- Handler Lambda code asset of the release: `@HANDLER_KEY@`
- Identity Lambda code asset of the release: `@IDENTITY_KEY@`
- Six release templates (`release-templates/`): the five `Ogevia-prd-*` stacks and the shared `Ogevia-prod-NetworkEgress`.

## Run (CloudShell, us-west-2)
```
tar -xzf ogevia-prd-release-discovery.tar.gz && cd ogevia-prd-release-discovery && sha256sum -c --quiet MANIFEST.sha256 && echo MANIFEST OK && cat BUILD_INFO.json
bash scripts/p0-baseline-capture.sh      # read-only; writes ~/ogevia-prd-before
bash scripts/p1-diff-report.sh           # local files only; compares live vs release templates
```
Needs `aws`, `jq`, `curl`, `python3`, `sha256sum`, `openssl`, `base64`. Output goes to `~/ogevia-prd-before/` (mode 700).

## Paste back
The whole terminal output of both scripts. The lines that matter most: the Lambda's CodeSha256, last-modified date and code-asset key; `version string` and `API host(s)` of the production web; the route count (the release has 228); the endpoint-policy line; and the `SUMMARY` line of every stack in p1.

## Stop rules
Any `STOP:` or `FAIL` line: paste the output; do not work around it. A `FAIL` in p0 means a read failed or a precondition (for example the Lambda serving ogevia_prd) did not hold; the report is still useful but the gate cannot be written until it is understood.

## What this stage does not do
It does not take a snapshot, open the database, migrate, deploy the API or web, change the shared security group, or touch TEST. Those belong to later stages and each needs its own approval.

## Stage B: the gate and the guard (added)
Stage A showed that five of the six stacks equal the release and that ONLY `Ogevia-prd-ServiceVerificationApi` differs (68 resources added: 66 routes and the ComplianceSweep rule and permission; 5 resources changed: both Lambda code assets, two role/function descriptions, and two IAM managed policies that each gain exactly one statement). Stage B turns that into checks:
```
bash scripts/p2-gate.sh          # read-only: re-reads production now, requires it to equal the p0 baseline, requires the other five stacks to equal the release, runs the expected-only gate on the API stack
bash scripts/deploy-guard-prd.sh check Ogevia-prd-ServiceVerificationApi     # same gate (+ the assembly check when OGEVIA_PRD_ASSEMBLY is set)
```
The gate (`scripts/prd_gate.py`) allows ONLY: the two pinned Lambda asset changes (from the observed live keys), the pinned description changes, one added statement `ClientDocumentObjects` (S3 get/get-version/put on the production evidence bucket's `*/client-documents/*/*`), one added statement `SwitchSignInsOnOff` (Cognito disable/enable on the single user pool), exactly 66 added routes (each JWT-authorized with an existing authorizer and an existing integration; a new public route is refused unless `OGEVIA_ALLOW_PUBLIC_ROUTES` names it after review), and the ComplianceSweep rule (rate(15 minutes), enabled, target the Handler) with its invoke permission. Anything else, or production not being in the recorded baseline state, prints `VIOLATION` lines and exits 1.
`deploy-guard-prd.sh deploy` exists but cannot run yet: it needs a deploy-mode synthesis made with the owner's approval reference, a pinned CDK CLI, a fresh snapshot named `ogevia-prod-postgres-pre-prdrelease-YYYYMMDDHHMM`, and a marker written by the migration stage after the database reaches 85/0085. Those stages are not part of this package. **Nothing in this package authorises or performs a deployment.**
Paste back: the output of `p2-gate.sh` (it prints every VIOLATION).


## Pinned values (every script refuses anything else)
- Migration set 0047-0085 sha256: `@MIGRATIONS_SHA@` (export as `OGEVIA_EXPECT_MIGRATIONS_SHA`)
- Rollback set 0047-0085 sha256: `@ROLLBACK_SHA@` (export as `OGEVIA_EXPECT_ROLLBACK_SHA`; only for a rollback)
- Release code commit (the TEST operator tarball `ogevia-operator-e80b770`): `e80b770210c54e423d28ecf48e00f1dda9506cec` (export as `OGEVIA_EXPECT_COMMIT` for D3 and E1). This package's own commit (above) is `OGEVIA_EXPECT_COMMIT` for D1.
- CDK CLI 2.1143.0 (from the operator package's own `node_modules`); Handler asset `@HANDLER_KEY@`; Identity asset `@IDENTITY_KEY@`.

